@@ -40,4 +40,4 @@ G-17. The developer agent edited files outside the write zone, or ran `git commi
 
 G-18. A research topic cut short (a session limit, a killed process) re-runs its skeptics when resumed with `resumeFromRunId`? The runtime's resume replays the unchanged prefix of agent calls and runs everything after the first divergence live, and the pipeline's call order after the drafts depends on which draft finished first, so a resumed topic replays the sources, the spine and the drafts and re-runs every first verdict. Continue from the journal instead: `scripts/research-continue.py --journal <transcript>/journal.jsonl --from-output <task output> --split 3` writes bundles that carry the finished stages as `resume_state` and draft only their own keys, so only the fixes and second verdicts run; `scripts/write-topic-result.py --run <cut-short output> --run <bundle output> ...` merges the runs into one note. A bundle above the runtime's 512 KiB script limit is refused: raise `--split`, since each bundle carries only its own keys' drafts and verdicts and no source layer. The second fix round is the same mechanism: `--round 2` with every journal of the first round names `fix:`/`verify2:` as the state and carries only the rules the second verdict sent back with `revise`; `--resume-cut-short` finishes a round whose fixes ran and whose skeptics were cut short; `--closing-edit` ships a rule the last verdict sent back after the drafter applies that verdict's edits verbatim; a topic's writer merge then lists the cut-short output and every bundle output of both rounds.
 
-G-19. A topic's workflow runs at two agents at a time and takes two hours? The runtime caps concurrency per workflow at `min(16, CPUs - 2)`, two on a four-CPU host. The cap is per workflow, so run several topics at once, and split one topic's draft-and-verify stage over several bundles through `scripts/research-continue.py --split`; the writer merges them.
+G-19. A topic's workflow runs at two agents at a time and takes two hours, or a review of a MEDIUM diff runs its ninety finders in series? The runtime caps concurrency per workflow at `min(16, CPUs - 2)`, two on a four-CPU host. The cap is per workflow, so run several workflows at once: for a topic, split its draft-and-verify stage over several bundles through `scripts/research-continue.py --split`, and the writer merges them; for a review, bundle the find and the verify stages in parts (`scripts/bundle-run.py --stage find --part <i>/<n>`, then `--stage aggregate --raw ...`, then `--stage verify --part <i>/<n>`) and join the verify parts with `scripts/merge-parts.py`, as SKILL.md §Phase 5 lays out. A part's output is a task output like any other; the aggregate stage reads the find parts' outputs whole.
